/T (Net Health Benefit \(NHB\) or Net Monetary Benefit \(NMB\) assumes that health has a particular value. Using this assumption, we can calculate the monetary loss of choosing strategies with lower health outcomes. Note that these formulas rely on a set willingness-to-pay threshold \(pg. 20\). NHB and NMB can be calculated by strategy or incrementally. The conclusions from either are identical.)
/E (Net Health Benefit \(NHB\) or Net Monetary Benefit \(NMB\) assumes that health has a particular value. Using this assumption, we can calculate the monetary loss of choosing strategies with lower health outcomes. Note that these formulas rely on a set willingness-to-pay threshold \(pg. 20\). NHB and NMB can be calculated by strategy or incrementally. The conclusions from either are identical.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [72]
